      "side": 1,
      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n---\n\nAcross the codebase, you frequently check `std::expected` for validity, and then call `.value()` to extract the underlying object.\n\n```c\nauto flash_res \u003d brom.GetFlashInfo();\nif (flash_res) {\n  auto \u0026flash \u003d flash_res.value();\n  std::println(\"  Product Name: {}\", MakeStringView(flash.prod_name));\n}\n\n```\n\n**The Fix:** `std::expected` intentionally implements pointer-like semantics via `operator*` and `operator-\u003e`. Leveraging these makes the code much more concise and visually distinguishes error-handling from data extraction.\n\nYou can collapse variable declarations and clean up the syntax everywhere:\n\n```c\nif (auto flash \u003d brom.GetFlashInfo()) {\n  std::println(\"  Product Name: {}\", MakeStringView(flash-\u003eprod_name));\n}\n\n```\n\nSimilarly, anywhere you are currently doing:\n\n```c\nauto ret_res \u003d DoUsbTransfer(...);\nif (!ret_res) return std::unexpected(ret_res.error());\nauto \u0026ret \u003d ret_res.value();\nreturn std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e(ret.begin(), ret.end());\n\n```\n\nIt can become a much cleaner:\n\n```c\nauto ret \u003d DoUsbTransfer(...);\nif (!ret) return std::unexpected(ret.error());\nreturn std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e(ret-\u003ebegin(), ret-\u003eend());\n\n```",
