      "writtenOn": "2026-06-15T22:29:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n---\n\nIn `cli_options_test.cc`, you wrote a very clever variadic template to wrap the C-style `getopt` parsing:\n\n```c\ntemplate \u003ctypename... Args\u003e\nstd::optional\u003cAppConfig\u003e RunParseArgs(Args... args) {\n  std::array\u003cchar*, sizeof...(args)\u003e argv \u003d {const_cast\u003cchar*\u003e(args)...};\n  return ParseArgs(argv.size(), argv.data());\n}\n\n```\n\n**The Issue:** The POSIX specification for `getopt` and `getopt_long` requires that the `argv` array is explicitly terminated by a null pointer (`argv[argc] \u003d\u003d NULL`). Your array is exactly the size of the arguments, meaning there is no trailing null pointer. Depending on the standard library implementation of `getopt`, this can read past the bounds of the array and invoke Undefined Behavior.\n\n**The Fix:** Expand the array by 1 and explicitly provide the `nullptr`.\n\n```c\ntemplate \u003ctypename... Args\u003e\nstd::optional\u003cAppConfig\u003e RunParseArgs(Args... args) {\n  // Add +1 to the size, and a trailing nullptr\n  std::array\u003cchar*, sizeof...(args) + 1\u003e argv \u003d {const_cast\u003cchar*\u003e(args)..., nullptr};\n  \n  // Pass size - 1 for argc\n  return ParseArgs(argv.size() - 1, argv.data());\n}\n\n```",
