      "side": 1,
      "message": "Gemini feedback:\n\n----\n\nIn `ec_protocol_defs.h`, you calculate the EC checksum using a raw `for` loop:\n\n```c\n  uint32_t sum \u003d 0;\n  for (uint8_t byte : packet) {\n    sum +\u003d byte;\n  }\n  packet[kChecksumOffset] \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n\n```\n\n**The Issue:** While perfectly functional, modern C++ philosophy (and Google Style) strongly encourages replacing raw `for` loops with standard algorithms whenever possible to explicitly declare *intent*. Computing the sum of a range is the textbook use case for `\u003cnumeric\u003e`.\n\n**The Fix:** Include `\u003cnumeric\u003e` and use `std::accumulate`. It reduces the calculation to a safe, declarative one-liner:\n\n```c\n#include \u003cnumeric\u003e\n\ninline void ApplyEcChecksum(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e packet) {\n  constexpr size_t kChecksumOffset \u003d offsetof(EcHostCommandRequest, checksum);\n  if (packet.size() \u003c\u003d kChecksumOffset) return;\n\n  packet[kChecksumOffset] \u003d 0;\n\n  int sum \u003d std::accumulate(packet.begin(), packet.end(), 0);\n  \n  packet[kChecksumOffset] \u003d static_cast\u003cuint8_t\u003e(-sum);\n}\n\n```",
